 The slot is claimed by the first activation only; subagent sessions re-activate this extension in the same process, and unconditionally overwriting would point the registry at a short-lived child manager whose shutdown would then delete the root session's entry ([#128](https://github.com/tintinweb/pi-subagents/pull/128)). Child activations leave it alone, and shutdown releases it only if this activation claimed it (`src/index.ts:747-750`, `:1105-1107`).
 
 Prefer the bus. The registry has no reply envelope, no version, and no availability event — `globalThis[Symbol.for("pi-subagents:manager")] === undefined` is the only probe you get, and it is also `undefined` in a session that filtered pi-subagents out. Reach for it for the two things the bus has no verb for — *is anything still running*, and *give me a settled record back* — or for a headless host that wants to block on `waitForAll()` before exiting.
 
+### Conditional retained-idle-child configuration
+
+These three **registry-only** methods require Pi's public `getConfigurationSnapshot`, `configureIfIdle`, `getConfigurationReceipt` session methods and root-package `resolveModelScopeFromModels` export. They return `unsupported` when any capability is absent; ordinary spawning/loading remains supported on the pinned Pi 0.84.2 development dependency. There is no raw-setter or private-runtime fallback. This does not add a bus channel or change protocol version 2.
+
+Concrete structural types are exported from `src/idle-child-configuration.ts`:
+
+```typescript
+type ThinkingLevel = "off" | "minimal" | "low" | "medium" | "high" | "xhigh" | "max";
+interface ChildConfigurationSnapshot {
+  readonly sessionId: string;
+  readonly revision: number;
+  readonly model: Readonly<{ provider: string; id: string }> | undefined;
+  readonly thinkingLevel: ThinkingLevel;
+}
+type ChildConfigurationReason =
+  | "busy" | "disposed" | "stale" | "invalid" | "unauthorized" | "cancelled"
+  | "operation-conflict" | "capacity" | "storage" | "unsupported"
+  | "not-found" | "scope-unknown" | "scope-denied";
+type ChildConfigurationSnapshotResult =
+  | { status: "ready"; childId: string; snapshot: ChildConfigurationSnapshot }
+  | { status: "rejected"; reason: ChildConfigurationReason };
+interface ConfigureIdleChildRequest {
+  operationId: string;
+  childId: string;
+  expectedSessionId: string;
+  expectedRevision: number;
+  model: { provider: string; id: string };
+  thinking: ThinkingLevel;
+  signal?: AbortSignal;
+}
+interface ChildConfigurationReceiptRequest {
+  operationId: string;
+  childId: string;
+  expectedSessionId: string;
+}
+interface ChildConfigurationReceipt {
+  readonly status: "committed" | "noop";
+  readonly operationId: string;
+  readonly before: ChildConfigurationSnapshot;
+  readonly after: ChildConfigurationSnapshot;
+  readonly revision: number;
+  readonly notificationErrors: readonly string[];
+}
+type ChildConfigurationResult = ChildConfigurationReceipt |
+  { status: "rejected"; operationId: string; reason: ChildConfigurationReason };
+```
+
+Snapshot and receipt lookup are synchronous. `configureIdleChild` calls Pi's atomic operation once, without waiting for idle, enqueuing a switch, aborting work, altering defaults, or launching another child. Pi owns transcript publication and pair/revision atomicity. `noop` is not a committed switch; `notificationErrors` report post-commit delivery failures, never a rollback. The ready snapshot is Pi's immutable value, not an idle reservation: native work may start before the next call, producing a refusal.
+
+Only completed, retained **top-level** records owned by the currently active root session are controllable. Actual `ctx.sessionManager.getSessionId()` and session-manager identity captured at spawn establish ownership; caller `rootSessionId` is lineage only. Missing ownership refuses control without breaking ordinary spawning. Missing/removed children return `not-found`; non-completed, owned-by-another-root/workflow/agent, pending-steer, queued, active, replaced, or disposed manager records return `unauthorized`. Pi can additionally refuse native activity as `busy`/`stale` or SDK disposal as `disposed`. The same authorization gates receipt exposure and retries.
+
+Authorization is repeated synchronously at Pi's commit boundary: registry identity, active context/epoch, root identity, same record and SDK instance, session ID/revision, manager admission generation, completed status, pending steers and queues, and fresh model scope. Manager admission generations change **before** foreground/background resume, queue/start, steer, abort, replacement/removal and disposal, so even a run that finishes during authentication invalidates an old attempt. `session_before_switch` invalidates the epoch immediately; a cancelled switch retains the active context, so a new attempt still works. Rebinding and shutdown invalidate it too. This is cooperative synchronization, not isolation from hostile in-process extensions or arbitrary direct record/SDK mutations.
+
+Scope is **mandatory for these explicit controls**, independently of the optional legacy `scopeModels` spawn policy. It reads the actual parent's captured configuration cwd and public `getAgentDir()` on every check, with the project `enabledModels` field overriding global. Absent files/fields and explicit `[]` mean unrestricted; malformed, unreadable or wrongly typed settings mean `scope-unknown`. Nonempty patterns use Pi's synchronous public resolver against the full current `registry.getAvailable?.() ?? registry.getAll()` universe. Globs and Pi's other pattern forms work; any diagnostic, unmatched pattern or restricted-empty result refuses with `scope-denied`. No scope cache or router candidate subset is used. Settings/registry changes during authentication are rechecked, not silently inherited. Existing launch scope behavior is unchanged.
+
+Use a nonempty operation ID of at most 256 characters. The wrapper retains a stable request and validator for every Pi-admitted ID; retries must have identical child/session/revision/model/thinking values **and the same signal object**. Conflicting reuse returns `operation-conflict`. In-flight duplicates coalesce; a committed receipt can replay during notification delivery without waiting on itself. New IDs are refused at 1024 cached operations per retained record, never silently evicted. Noop and refused Pi operations also occupy capacity. Receipts and IDs are process-local, not recovered from disk. A scope/ownership change can deny access to an existing receipt; denial of receipt access does not mean a previous commit was undone.
+
+```typescript
+const ready = registry.getIdleChildConfigurationSnapshot(childId);
+if (ready.status === "ready") {
+  const result = await registry.configureIdleChild({
+    operationId: crypto.randomUUID(), childId,
+    expectedSessionId: ready.snapshot.sessionId,
+    expectedRevision: ready.snapshot.revision,
+    model: { provider: "local", id: "review-model" }, thinking: "off",
+  });
+  // Inspect result.status; never infer a switch from the requested pair.
+}
+```
+
+`test/idle-child-configuration.test.ts` and `test/idle-child-registry.test.ts` are **unit** tests: real manager/registry/lifecycle with fake atomic sessions and scope resolver boundaries. They do not prove Pi transcript atomicity, native pattern resolution, or real provider authentication.
+
 ## Protocol versions
 
 `subagents:rpc:ping` replies `{ version: PROTOCOL_VERSION }`, currently `2` (`src/cross-extension-rpc.ts:33`). The constant was introduced already equal to `2` in 0.5.0; "v1" is a retroactive name for the pre-envelope contract, where spawn replied with a bare `{ id }` or `{ error }`, stop replied `{ success: boolean }` with no message, and each handler caught its own errors.
